---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { LAST_UPDATED } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate, formatDecimal } from '../../lib/format';
import { P, SNAPSHOT, type SourceKey } from '../../lib/engine/params';
import { OZ, TOLA, coinById, coinFineGrams } from '../../lib/engine/metal';
import { stamp } from '../../lib/money';
const lang = 'en';
const L = (k: SourceKey, t?: string) => `<a href="${P.sources[k].url}" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">${t ?? P.sources[k].label.en}</a>`;
const sov = coinById('sovereign');
---
<PageLayout title="Gold by Carat Method: Spot Price, Fineness, Coins and Tax" description="How Gold by Carat works out metal values: the gold-api.com spot price and its time, UK legal fineness, Coinage Act weights, Royal Mint data, CGT and VAT." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the values are worked out', description: 'Method of Gold by Carat', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">How the values are worked out</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>Every calculator calls one engine of pure functions, fed by a parameter file of legal standards, coin weights and tax rules, and by the spot price. No amount is typed into a page. The parameter file was last checked against its sources on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}; it is updated by hand when a law, an HMRC rate or a Royal Mint specification changes.</p>
    <h2>1. The spot price</h2>
    <p>The price of a troy ounce of gold, silver, platinum and palladium in pounds comes from gold-api.com, which publishes it for each metal directly in GBP. Its terms, read on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-GB')}, allow commercial use, forbid several requests per second and give no guarantee of accuracy. We use it as follows:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>when the site is built, one request per metal, spaced more than a second apart, is saved with its time; the text of each page and the first display of each calculator use that price, and say when it was recorded (the current file: gold <span set:html={stamp(SNAPSHOT.at.XAU)} />);</li>
      <li>when you open a page, your browser asks gold-api.com for the price of each metal shown on it, one request at a time at least 1.2 seconds apart, and the calculator shows the time that price carries;</li>
      <li>if that request fails, the calculator keeps the build price and says so, with its date and time;</li>
      <li>nothing refreshes by itself; the “Check for a newer price” button asks again, at most once every 15 seconds.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>No input you type is sent with these requests: they are plain public requests with no parameters. We do not promise any particular freshness, because it depends on gold-api.com.</p>
    <h2>2. Metal value</h2>
    <p>Value = weight in grams × fineness ÷ 1,000 × spot price per troy ounce ÷ {formatDecimal(OZ, 7)}. The troy ounce is 0.0311034768 kg and the grain 1/7,000 of a pound of 0.45359237 kg, per <Fragment set:html={L('ukWeights', 'Schedule 1 of the Weights and Measures Act 1985')} />. The tola is taken as {P.units.tolaGrains} grains ({formatDecimal(TOLA, 4)} g), a South Asian trade convention with no British legal definition, and the calculator lets you change it.</p>
    <h2>3. Fineness by carat</h2>
    <p>Carats are converted with the standards of <Fragment set:html={L('hallmarkSch2', 'Schedule 2 of the Hallmarking Act 1973')} /> and the carat table of <Fragment set:html={L('hallmarkSch1', 'Schedule 1')} />: 9ct = 375, 14ct = 585, 18ct = 750, 22ct = 916.6. 24ct is valued at 999, the higher of the two pure gold standards (990 and 999). 21ct, which is not a UK standard, is valued at 875 (21/24) as a guide. Any other carat in the converter is k/24 and is labelled as not a UK standard.</p>
    <h2>4. Coins</h2>
    <p>Sovereign, half, double and £5 coins: standard weight and eleven twelfths fineness from <Fragment set:html={L('coinageAct', 'Schedule 1 of the Coinage Act 1971 (as enacted)')} />. The sovereign’s {formatDecimal(sov.grossG!, 5)} g at 916.66 gives {formatDecimal(coinFineGrams(sov), 4)} g of fine gold, 0.2354 oz, which matches the Royal Mint’s 0.235 oz. The quarter sovereign, the gold Britannias (1, ½, ¼ and 1/10 oz at 999.9), the silver Britannia (1 oz at 999), the Krugerrand (1 oz at 916.7) and the Maple Leaf (1 oz at 999) use the fine content in the Royal Mint’s product specifications. Older coins of other specifications are not covered; we found no official source for the silver content of older British silver coins and do not show it.</p>
    <h2>5. Offers and premiums</h2>
    <p>Share of metal = offer ÷ metal value. Premium = price ÷ metal value − 1. These are arithmetic only; the site shows no buyer’s or dealer’s prices and no “typical” rate. The default percentage in the scrap calculator is a starting value to change, not a market figure.</p>
    <h2>6. Capital Gains Tax ({P.taxYear})</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>UK legal tender coins (sovereigns, Britannias): sterling currency, not a chargeable asset (<Fragment set:html={L('tcga21')} />).</li>
      <li>Jewellery, bars and foreign coins: chattels; exempt if sold for £{P.chattels.exemptLimit.toLocaleString('en-GB')} or less, gain otherwise capped at 5/3 of the excess (<Fragment set:html={L('tcga262')} />; <Fragment set:html={L('govChattels')} />). Foreign coins are chargeable (<Fragment set:html={L('cg78300')} />).</li>
      <li>Annual exempt amount £{P.cgt.annualExemptAmount.toLocaleString('en-GB')}; {Math.round(P.cgt.basicRate * 100)}% within the unused basic rate band of £{P.cgt.basicRateBand.toLocaleString('en-GB')}, {Math.round(P.cgt.higherRate * 100)}% above (<Fragment set:html={L('govCgtRates')} />).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The engine is tested against GOV.UK’s worked examples. It assumes no other gains in the year, does not apply loss relief and does not cover trusts, companies or non-residents.</p>
    <h2>7. VAT</h2>
    <p>Investment gold under <Fragment set:html={L('vatNotice70121')} />: bars of at least {P.vat.barMinFineness} thousandths; coins of at least {P.vat.coinMinFineness} thousandths, minted after {P.vat.coinMintedAfter}, legal tender in their country, normally sold at no more than {Math.round(P.vat.coinPremiumLimit * 100)}% of their gold value. Everything else is standard-rated at {Math.round(P.vat.standardRate * 100)}%. The calculator applies the 180% test to the price you type, as a guide; HMRC applies it to the coin’s normal selling price.</p>
    <h2>8. What is left out</h2>
    <p>Buyers’ and dealers’ prices; numismatic or antique value; stones and non-precious parts (you subtract them from the weight); historical prices (we have not confirmed a source we may republish); personal tax circumstances. Values are estimates of metal content, not offers or valuations.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-navy-600">Method last reviewed on <time datetime={LAST_UPDATED}>{displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-GB')}</time>.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
